(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xxi-b"] = {"qs": [["Introduction to Securities Markets", "The market capitalisation category of a stock for Indian mutual funds and many portfolio managers is based on:", ["The number of shareholders recorded in the company's register", "The face value of its shares and the size of its dividend", "Its ranking by full market capitalisation in AMFI's list", "The number of employees it has across all its offices"], 2, "AMFI publishes a half-yearly list ranking companies by full market cap: 1–100 large cap, 101–250 mid cap and the rest small cap. The industry widely uses this classification."], ["Risk", "Which risk measure is most relevant for an index fund manager?", ["Absolute VaR of the client's bank deposits", "Tracking error", "Credit rating of the custodian", "Number of trades per day"], 1, "An index fund aims to replicate its benchmark, so the key risk is deviation from the index return, measured by tracking error."], ["Equity Portfolio Management Strategies", "A contrarian equity manager is most likely to:", ["Buy the stocks with the strongest recent price momentum", "Buy out-of-favour stocks that others are selling", "Follow the consensus of analysts on every stock", "Hold only the largest stocks in the benchmark"], 1, "Contrarians believe markets overreact, so they buy unpopular, beaten-down stocks expecting a rebound as sentiment normalises. This approach is the opposite of momentum investing."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "An investor who wants a portfolio riskier than the market portfolio on the CML must:", ["Sell the market portfolio and hold only government bonds", "Hold only a single high-beta stock", "Borrow at the risk-free rate to buy more of the market", "Avoid the risk-free asset and pick stocks at random"], 2, "Points beyond the market portfolio on the CML are reached by leverage: borrowing at the risk-free rate to invest more than 100% in the market portfolio."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio manager receives a complaint from a client. Ideally it should:", ["Ignore it unless SEBI also receives the complaint", "Ask the client to approach the stock exchange first", "Close the client's account to avoid the complaint", "Resolve it promptly and record it"], 3, "Intermediaries must address complaints quickly, maintain records and report status. Unresolved complaints may be escalated by the client through SCORES and ODR."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "If a market is weak-form efficient, which statement is correct?", ["Chart patterns reliably forecast price movements", "Fundamental analysis is useless in all circumstances", "Past prices cannot predict future returns", "Prices already reflect all private information"], 2, "Weak-form efficiency means prices already reflect past trading data, so technical analysis cannot consistently earn excess returns. Fundamental analysis may still add value."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "The separation theorem states that:", ["Each investor must hold a completely different risky portfolio", "Investors should never combine risky assets with the risk-free asset", "Portfolio decisions should ignore the risk-free rate entirely", "All investors hold the same risky portfolio, varying only the risk-free share"], 3, "With a risk-free asset, the optimal risky portfolio is the same market portfolio for everyone. Risk preferences only decide how much to lend or borrow at the risk-free rate."], ["Risk", "Downside deviation is preferred to standard deviation by some investors because it:", ["Treats upside and downside surprises as equally bad", "Ignores all negative returns in the period", "Is always larger than standard deviation", "Counts only returns below a target"], 3, "Investors usually worry about falls, not gains. Downside deviation measures volatility below a minimum acceptable return, and is used in the Sortino ratio."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "If a portfolio manager fails to comply with SEBI regulations, SEBI can:", ["Only send an advisory letter with no further action possible", "Transfer the manager's clients to a mutual fund automatically", "Ask the clients to pay the penalty instead", "Impose penalties or suspend or cancel registration"], 3, "SEBI has wide powers of inspection, adjudication and enforcement, including monetary penalties, directions to clients' benefit, and suspension or cancellation of the portfolio manager's registration."], ["Risk", "Which risk would most affect an Indian investor holding US technology shares when the rupee strengthens against the dollar?", ["Reinvestment risk on bond coupons received during the year", "Currency risk", "Credit risk of the Indian government on its rupee bonds", "Liquidity risk in Indian small-cap stocks"], 1, "When the rupee strengthens, dollar assets are worth fewer rupees, reducing returns for an Indian investor. This is currency or exchange rate risk."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "Goal-based investing differs from a single-portfolio approach because it:", ["Puts all goals into one portfolio with one common risk level", "Ignores the time horizon of individual goals", "Focuses only on beating a benchmark index every year", "Builds separate allocations for each goal"], 3, "Goal-based investing segments wealth by goals such as education, home or retirement, each with its own horizon and risk. This can make allocations and progress clearer to clients."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "Which factor most increases a client's ability to take risk?", ["A large loan EMI due every month with uncertain income", "A planned large withdrawal within the next year", "Total dependence on the portfolio for daily living expenses", "A long horizon with stable income"], 3, "Risk capacity rises with longer horizons, stable income and lower liabilities. Heavy obligations, near-term withdrawals and dependence on the portfolio reduce capacity."], ["Introduction to Indices", "In a price-weighted index of three stocks priced ₹100, ₹200 and ₹700, a 10% rise in the ₹700 stock (others unchanged) raises the index by about:", ["10%", "7%", "3.3%", "0.7%"], 1, "In a price-weighted index, the ₹700 stock is 700/1,000 = 70% of the total. A 10% rise adds ₹70 to the ₹1,000 total, raising the index by 7%."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Corporate actions such as dividends and bonus shares on PMS holdings are:", ["Credited to the respective client's account", "Kept by the portfolio manager as part of its fee income", "Shared equally among all clients regardless of holdings", "Ignored because PMS accounts do not receive corporate actions"], 0, "Since securities are held in each client's name, dividends, bonuses and other benefits accrue to that client. The manager must reflect them in client statements."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "In a discretionary PMS, who decides which securities to buy and sell?", ["The client, who must approve every single trade in advance", "The portfolio manager, within the agreement", "The custodian, based on its own market view", "The distributor who introduced the client to the manager"], 1, "Under a discretionary mandate, the client gives the manager authority to take investment decisions within the agreed objectives and restrictions. The custodian and distributor do not decide trades."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Which of the following must a portfolio manager do under KYC and AML norms?", ["Skip KYC for clients investing above ₹1 crore", "Verify identity and monitor for suspicious transactions", "Rely only on the distributor's oral confirmation of identity", "Report clients' personal details publicly on its website"], 1, "Portfolio managers are reporting entities under PMLA and must carry out KYC, risk categorisation and transaction monitoring, and report suspicious transactions to FIU-IND."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "Which market segment is used by the Reserve Bank of India to conduct auctions of Government securities?", ["The primary market for G-secs", "The equity cash segment of a stock exchange", "The commodity derivatives segment of an exchange", "The SME platform of a stock exchange"], 0, "The RBI issues G-secs on behalf of the government through primary auctions. Secondary trading happens on platforms like NDS-OM and exchange debt segments."], ["Mutual Fund", "A scheme's total expense ratio (TER) is charged:", ["Daily, through a reduction in NAV", "Once a year as a separate invoice sent to each investor", "Only at the time of redemption as a lump sum", "Only on investors who invest through distributors"], 0, "TER is accrued daily and deducted from scheme assets, so the NAV reflects it. It applies to all investors in a plan; direct plans have a lower TER than regular plans."], ["Investing in Stocks", "Enterprise value (EV) of a company is generally calculated as:", ["Market cap − debt + cash and cash equivalents held", "Market cap + debt − cash", "Book value of equity + reserves − all current liabilities", "Net profit × dividend payout ratio × number of shares"], 1, "EV measures the value of the whole business to all capital providers. Debt is added because an acquirer must pay it off, and cash is subtracted because it reduces the net cost."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "Which measure would best show whether a manager's outperformance is consistent rather than due to a few big bets?", ["Absolute return over one month", "Total value of assets under management", "Number of stocks in the portfolio", "Information ratio"], 3, "Information ratio divides active return by tracking error, rewarding steady outperformance per unit of active risk. A high return from a few bets would come with high tracking error."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "An asset with a lower expected return than others may still earn a place in a mean-variance optimal portfolio because:", ["Its low correlation can reduce total risk", "Mean-variance optimisation always gives equal weights to every asset available", "Assets with low expected returns are guaranteed to rise in the next year", "SEBI requires every portfolio to hold at least one low-return asset"], 0, "In MPT what matters is an asset's contribution to portfolio risk and return. A low- or negatively correlated asset can lower overall volatility enough to improve the portfolio's risk-return trade-off."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "An upward-sloping (normal) yield curve indicates that:", ["Short-term yields are higher than long-term yields for all maturities", "All maturities offer exactly the same yield to investors", "Long-term yields are higher than short-term yields", "Bond prices are guaranteed to rise over the next year"], 2, "A normal yield curve slopes upward because investors usually demand extra yield for longer maturities. An inverted curve, with short yields above long yields, often signals slowing growth."], ["Derivatives", "Option theta measures:", ["Loss of option value as time passes", "The change in option price for a change in interest rates", "The change in delta for a change in the underlying price", "The change in option price for a change in volatility"], 0, "Theta captures time decay — options lose value as expiry nears, all else equal. Rho relates to interest rates, gamma to changes in delta and vega to volatility."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "When the PM buys a stock for many discretionary clients in one block order, the shares must be allocated:", ["First to clients who pay the highest management fees", "Only to the client accounts of the PM's own employees", "Fairly as per a pre-defined allocation policy", "Randomly, without keeping any records of the allocation"], 2, "Block trades must be allocated according to a documented fair-allocation policy with records. Favouring higher-fee or related accounts is a conflict of interest."], ["Derivatives", "A portfolio worth ₹5 crore has a beta of 0.8. The Nifty futures contract value is ₹20 lakh. The number of contracts to sell for a full hedge is:", ["25", "16", "20", "40"], 2, "Hedge value = portfolio × beta = ₹5 crore × 0.8 = ₹4 crore. Contracts = ₹4 crore ÷ ₹20 lakh = 20. Ignoring beta would suggest 25 contracts, over-hedging the portfolio."], ["Behavioral Finance", "After a strong two-year rally, a client wants to put all savings into equities because 'markets always go up'. The bias most evident is:", ["Recency bias", "Loss aversion leading to excessive caution", "Endowment effect over inherited property", "Conservatism in updating beliefs"], 0, "Recency bias overweights recent experience and extrapolates it into the future. The manager should remind the client about cycles and stick to the agreed allocation."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Before a portfolio manager changes the fee structure for an existing client, it should:", ["Simply deduct the new fees from the next quarter onwards", "Inform only the distributor, who will tell the client later", "Publish the change on its website and assume consent", "Obtain the client's consent and amend the agreement"], 3, "Fees are part of the client agreement, so changes require the client's informed consent. Deducting revised fees without agreement breaches the contract and SEBI norms."], ["Investment Landscape", "Which asset class generally offers the highest liquidity with the lowest price risk?", ["Cash and short-term money-market instruments", "Unlisted shares of a private company in a growing sector", "A commercial office property in a metro city", "Listed small-cap equity shares with low trading volume"], 0, "Cash and money-market instruments can be converted quickly with little change in value. Unlisted shares, property and thinly traded small caps are slow or costly to sell."], ["Derivatives", "Vega of an option is highest for options that are:", ["At the money with longer time to expiry", "Deep in the money and expiring the same day", "Deep out of the money and expiring the same day", "Already exercised by the buyer"], 0, "Vega measures sensitivity to volatility. At-the-money options with more time remaining have the most time value and therefore respond most to changes in volatility."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "A client invests ₹10 lakh, which grows to ₹11 lakh after 6 months; he adds ₹9 lakh, and the portfolio ends the year at ₹21 lakh. The time-weighted return for the year is:", ["10.0%", "5.0%", "15.5%", "11.0%"], 2, "Sub-period 1: 11/10 = 1.10. Sub-period 2: start 11 + 9 = 20, end 21, so 1.05. TWR = 1.10 × 1.05 − 1 = 15.5%. TWR removes the effect of the client's added money."], ["Derivatives", "A 'collar' on a stock holding is created by:", ["Selling a put and buying a call on the stock at the same strike", "Buying futures and simultaneously buying a call option", "Selling the stock and buying a government bond with the proceeds", "Buying a put and selling a call on the stock"], 3, "A collar buys downside protection (put) and funds it partly by selling upside (call). The result is a range of outcomes bounded on both sides."], ["Investment Landscape", "Over the long run, the main reason an investor accepts equity volatility is:", ["Equity prices are protected by SEBI against falls of more than 10% a year", "Equity dividends are fixed in advance like bank deposit interest", "The potential to earn returns above inflation", "Equity markets in India never fall over any three-year period"], 2, "Equity offers ownership in growing businesses, giving potential returns above inflation over long periods. Prices are not protected, dividends are not fixed and multi-year losses can occur."], ["Fixed Income Portfolio Management Strategies", "Cash-flow matching for a pension fund involves:", ["Holding equities that are expected to grow faster than liabilities", "Buying bonds whose cash flows match each liability", "Keeping the entire portfolio in a savings account", "Trading bonds daily to capture price movements"], 1, "Cash-flow matching dedicates bond coupons and maturities to meet specific future payments, minimising reinvestment and interest rate risk on those liabilities."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Portfolio managers must get each client's portfolio and accounts audited:", ["Only when the client asks for an audit in writing", "Annually by an independent chartered accountant", "Once every five years by the stock exchange", "Daily by the client's distributor"], 1, "SEBI requires annual audit of client accounts by an independent chartered accountant, with a copy of the certificate given to the client. This independently verifies holdings and fees."], ["Mutual Fund", "A Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP) is most useful for an investor who wants to:", ["Invest a fixed amount every month from salary into equity schemes", "Shift money from a debt scheme to an equity scheme in instalments", "Receive regular cash flows from investments", "Lock in money for three years for tax benefits under the scheme"], 2, "SWP redeems units periodically to provide regular income. SIP invests regularly, STP transfers between schemes and ELSS carries a lock-in."], ["Portfolio Rebalancing", "Using index futures to rebalance a large portfolio temporarily is attractive because it:", ["Removes all market risk from the portfolio permanently", "Avoids the need for any margin payments", "Changes exposure quickly at low cost", "Is the only rebalancing method permitted by SEBI"], 2, "Futures let managers adjust equity exposure rapidly without selling many individual stocks. They require margins and must be managed within regulatory limits."], ["Behavioral Finance", "Prospect theory suggests that investors:", ["Always make decisions that maximise their expected wealth", "Feel gains and losses of the same size with equal intensity", "Prefer certain losses to uncertain losses in all cases", "Feel losses more strongly than equal gains"], 3, "Kahneman and Tversky showed that losses hurt roughly twice as much as equal gains please, and people evaluate outcomes relative to a reference point. This explains many investment errors."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "A 'stop-loss' sell order is generally used to:", ["Buy more shares automatically whenever the price rises by a set amount", "Guarantee that shares are sold at exactly the trigger price in all conditions", "Limit losses if the price falls to a trigger level", "Cancel all pending orders at the end of the trading day"], 2, "A stop-loss order becomes active when the trigger price is hit and then sells, limiting further losses. In fast markets it may execute below the trigger, so the price is not guaranteed."], ["Taxation", "Interest received on corporate bonds held in a PMS account is taxed as:", ["Long-term capital gains at a concessional rate", "Exempt income because bonds are listed", "Income at the client's slab rate", "Business income of the portfolio manager"], 2, "Interest is taxed as income from other sources at the client's applicable slab rate, regardless of whether the bond is listed. Gains on sale of the bond are taxed separately as capital gains."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A discretionary portfolio manager can invest a client's funds in unlisted securities only:", ["Without any limit, if the manager believes in the company", "Only if the unlisted company is owned by the manager's group", "Never, because unlisted securities are banned for all PMS", "Up to the SEBI-specified limit, with disclosure"], 3, "SEBI caps discretionary PMS investment in unlisted securities as a share of client AUM. Non-discretionary and advisory mandates may follow client instructions within norms."], ["Fixed Income Portfolio Management Strategies", "A laddered bond portfolio is useful for investors who want to:", ["Make a large bet on a single maturity point", "Avoid any interest rate risk completely", "Spread reinvestment risk with regular maturities", "Hold only long-term bonds"], 2, "A ladder holds bonds maturing at regular intervals, so some money is reinvested each period at prevailing rates, smoothing reinvestment and rate risk."], ["Introduction to Indices", "Which feature makes an index 'investable'?", ["It contains only unlisted companies that rarely trade", "Its weights are changed secretly without any announcement", "Its constituents are liquid and replicable", "It is computed from the fund manager's own estimates of value"], 2, "An investable index uses liquid securities and transparent rules so that a fund can actually replicate it. This makes it a fair benchmark and a basis for passive products."], ["Portfolio Rebalancing", "A constant-mix strategy tends to perform best in markets that:", ["Trend strongly upwards for many years without reversal", "Fluctuate up and down without a clear trend", "Trend strongly downwards for many years without reversal", "Have zero volatility at all times"], 1, "Constant-mix sells after rises and buys after falls, which pays off when prices oscillate. In strong trends it underperforms buy-and-hold, as it keeps selling winners."], ["Fixed Income Portfolio Management Strategies", "Immunisation of a bond portfolio is achieved by setting:", ["Portfolio duration to twice the liability horizon", "Portfolio duration equal to the liability horizon", "Portfolio duration to zero by holding cash", "Portfolio maturity to the longest bond available"], 1, "Matching duration to the horizon balances price risk against reinvestment risk, so the portfolio value at the horizon is protected against small parallel rate changes."], ["Behavioral Finance", "Which practice best helps a portfolio manager reduce the impact of behavioural biases on decisions?", ["A rules-based process with written criteria", "Making decisions quickly based on gut feeling during market hours", "Following the most popular stock ideas on social media", "Reviewing only information that supports existing holdings"], 0, "Checklists, documented investment cases and independent review make decisions more consistent and expose biases like confirmation and overconfidence. Gut feeling and herding amplify them."], ["Investment Landscape", "Liquidity risk for an investor means the risk that:", ["The company issuing the asset will stop all business operations", "An asset cannot be sold quickly without a price concession", "Inflation will be higher than the return on the asset", "Interest rates will fall after the asset is bought"], 1, "Liquidity risk concerns the ability to convert an asset to cash at a fair price when needed. Business failure is credit or business risk; inflation and rate changes are separate risks."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "A 45-year-old client in the 30% tax bracket asks how taxes affect asset allocation. The most relevant consideration is:", ["Post-tax returns of each asset class", "Only the pre-tax returns shown in fund factsheets", "Only the asset class that gave the highest return last year", "The number of securities in each asset class"], 0, "Taxes reduce realised returns differently across assets and holding periods. Comparing post-tax expected returns helps choose an efficient allocation for the client."], ["Mutual Fund", "Which mutual fund category is designed to track an index with minimal deviation?", ["Focused funds holding up to 30 high-conviction stocks", "Contra funds that buy out-of-favour sectors", "Dynamic asset allocation funds changing equity exposure", "Index funds and ETFs"], 3, "Passive funds replicate an index, aiming for low tracking error and low costs. Focused, contra and dynamic funds are actively managed."], ["Behavioral Finance", "Investors judge a company to be a great investment simply because it has a great product they love. This is an example of:", ["Mental accounting of different money pots", "Representativeness bias", "Regret aversion about missed opportunities", "Status quo bias in portfolio allocation"], 1, "Representativeness leads people to judge by stereotypes or similarity: a good product feels like a good stock. But a stock's return depends on price paid relative to value."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "Which of the following is regulated primarily by the RBI rather than SEBI?", ["Stock brokers in the equity cash segment", "The government securities and money markets", "Mutual funds and their asset management companies", "Portfolio managers registered under the 2020 Regulations"], 1, "RBI regulates the G-sec, money and foreign exchange markets, along with banks and NBFCs. Brokers, mutual funds and portfolio managers are regulated by SEBI."], ["Derivatives", "A covered call strategy involves:", ["Buying a call option without owning the underlying shares", "Holding a stock and selling a call on it", "Selling a put option and holding cash to cover assignment", "Buying both a call and a put at the same strike price"], 1, "The investor owns the shares and writes calls against them to earn premium, accepting a cap on upside. Buying a call and put together is a straddle."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "An investor sees a stock's 'circuit filter' hit on the upper side. This means:", ["The stock has been permanently delisted from the stock exchange", "The company has announced a bonus issue for all shareholders", "Trading is restricted beyond a set price band for the day", "The stock's price is guaranteed to rise further the next day"], 2, "Price bands limit how far a stock can move in a day to curb volatility. Once the band is hit, trades cannot happen beyond it until the next session or band revision."], ["Equity Portfolio Management Strategies", "Sector rotation as an active strategy involves:", ["Holding every sector at exactly its index weight at all times", "Investing only in a single sector for the life of the portfolio", "Buying stocks with the lowest trading volume in each sector", "Overweighting sectors expected to do well in the next phase of the cycle"], 3, "Sector rotation shifts weights across sectors based on economic or market views, for example favouring defensives in slowdowns and cyclicals in recoveries."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "A zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration is:", ["Equal to its time to maturity", "Always zero, because it does not pay any coupon at all", "Half of its time to maturity in all cases", "Longer than its time to maturity"], 0, "A zero-coupon bond pays a single cash flow at maturity, so its weighted average time to cash flow equals its maturity. Coupon bonds have durations shorter than maturity."], ["Portfolio Rebalancing", "A buy-and-hold strategy, compared with constant-mix, has a pay-off that is:", ["Concave, gaining in oscillating markets", "Convex, protecting a fixed floor value", "Always identical to CPPI's pay-off", "Linear in the risky asset's return"], 3, "Buy-and-hold never trades, so its value moves linearly with the risky asset. Constant-mix is concave and CPPI convex, offering protection in falling markets."], ["Taxation", "Why do portfolio managers often track 'tax lots' in client portfolios?", ["To decide the number of shares a company may issue", "To compute gains using correct cost and dates", "To set the price band for a stock on the exchange", "To calculate the market capitalisation of an index"], 1, "Each purchase creates a tax lot with its own cost and date. Tracking lots lets the manager compute gains accurately and choose sales that are more tax-efficient."], ["Derivatives", "An option's delta of 0.6 means that, for a small ₹1 rise in the underlying price, the option premium will change by about:", ["₹6.00", "₹1.60", "₹0.60", "₹0.06"], 2, "Delta measures the sensitivity of the option price to the underlying. A delta of 0.6 implies about ₹0.60 change for a ₹1 move in the underlying, for small changes."], ["Behavioral Finance", "A client keeps 'safe money' in a fixed deposit earning 6% while carrying a credit card balance at 36%. This reflects:", ["Herding behaviour driven by the actions of other investors", "Overconfidence in his own stock-picking ability", "Mental accounting", "Hindsight bias about past market events"], 2, "Mental accounting treats money in separate 'buckets' instead of as a whole. Paying off the expensive loan with the deposit would clearly improve his overall position."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "Two assets have standard deviations of 15% and 25% and a correlation of zero. The standard deviation of a 50:50 portfolio is approximately:", ["14.6%", "20.0%", "10.0%", "17.5%"], 0, "σ = √(0.5² × 15² + 0.5² × 25²) = √(56.25 + 156.25) = √212.5 ≈ 14.6%. Low correlation gives risk below the 20% weighted average."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "State Development Loans (SDLs) are bonds issued by:", ["State governments", "Public sector banks for funding home loans to individuals", "The Central Government to fund the Union budget deficit", "Municipal corporations to fund local water projects only"], 0, "SDLs are market borrowings of state governments, auctioned through the RBI. They usually yield slightly more than Central Government securities of similar maturity."], ["Introduction to Indices", "A total return index for equities assumes that:", ["Dividends are ignored and only price movement counts", "Dividends are reinvested in the index", "All constituents have exactly the same weight", "Prices are adjusted for inflation every month"], 1, "TRI includes dividends reinvested, so it reflects the full return an investor would earn. SEBI requires mutual funds and PMS to benchmark against total return variants."], ["Introduction to Indices", "An index starts with a base value of 1,000. If the total free-float market value of its constituents rises 12% from the base date, the index value is:", ["1,120", "1,012", "1,200", "112"], 0, "A market-cap weighted index moves in proportion to the aggregate free-float value: 1,000 × 1.12 = 1,120. The base value simply sets the starting level."], ["Portfolio Rebalancing", "A ₹1 crore portfolio targets 50% equity and 50% debt. After a rally, equity is ₹62 lakh and debt ₹48 lakh. To rebalance, the manager should:", ["Sell ₹7 lakh of equity and buy ₹7 lakh of debt", "Sell ₹12 lakh of equity and buy ₹12 lakh of debt", "Buy ₹7 lakh of equity and sell ₹7 lakh of debt", "Sell ₹14 lakh of equity and hold it in cash"], 0, "Total = ₹110 lakh, so the target is ₹55 lakh each. Equity must fall by 62 − 55 = ₹7 lakh and debt must rise by 55 − 48 = ₹7 lakh."], ["Introduction to Indices", "A debt fund manager wants a benchmark for a portfolio of short-term corporate bonds. A suitable choice is:", ["The Nifty 50 Total Return Index of large-cap equities", "A gold price index tracking international bullion prices", "A short-term corporate bond index", "A small-cap equity index of recently listed companies"], 2, "Benchmarks should reflect the portfolio's asset class, maturity and credit profile. An equity or gold index would not measure a bond manager's skill."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "Employees of a portfolio manager involved in investment decision-making are required to:", ["Hold a separate SEBI registration as research analysts in all cases", "Also register with IRDAI as insurance agents", "Obtain the specified NISM certification", "Have at least 15 years of experience in banking"], 2, "SEBI requires the principal officer and employees in decision-making roles to hold the relevant NISM certification (Series XXI-B) to ensure minimum knowledge standards."], ["Introduction to Indices", "Index rebalancing at regular intervals mainly helps to:", ["Guarantee that the index will outperform active managers", "Keep the index aligned with its methodology", "Freeze constituents so they never change over time", "Remove all price volatility from the index"], 1, "Over time, price changes and corporate events cause drift. Periodic rebalancing restores weights and eligibility per the rules so the index keeps representing its target segment."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio earned 16% with standard deviation 20%; the market earned 12% with standard deviation 15%; the risk-free rate is 6%. The portfolio's M-squared (M²) is:", ["16.0%", "12.0%", "13.5%", "10.0%"], 2, "Sharpe = (16 − 6)/20 = 0.5. M² = Rf + Sharpe × σm = 6 + 0.5 × 15 = 13.5%. It beats the market's 12% on a risk-adjusted basis, despite higher volatility."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A discounted cash flow (DCF) valuation is most sensitive to:", ["The discount rate and terminal growth assumptions", "The company's face value per share and its listing date", "The number of shareholders who attended the last AGM", "The colour and design of the company's annual report"], 0, "Most DCF value often lies in the terminal value, which depends heavily on long-term growth and the discount rate. Small changes in these inputs can change the valuation substantially."], ["Risk", "Inflation risk is greatest for which investor?", ["A young investor holding a diversified equity portfolio for 20 years", "A retiree with long-term fixed-income bonds", "An investor holding inflation-indexed government bonds", "A business owner whose prices rise with inflation"], 1, "Fixed nominal payments lose purchasing power when inflation rises. Equity and inflation-indexed bonds offer some protection, as do businesses able to raise prices."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "A client requires a 9% annual return to meet her retirement goal but has low risk tolerance. The portfolio manager should first:", ["Invest aggressively to reach 9% regardless of her low tolerance", "Promise her 9% so she remains comfortable with the plan", "Discuss trade-offs and possibly revise the goal", "Ignore her risk tolerance because the goal is more important"], 2, "When required return and risk tolerance conflict, the manager must explain the trade-offs: save more, extend the horizon or lower the target. Ignoring tolerance or promising returns is unsuitable."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "As more randomly chosen stocks are added to a portfolio, total portfolio risk:", ["Falls to zero once the portfolio holds about 30 stocks", "Rises steadily because each stock adds its own risk", "Falls towards the level of market risk", "Stays exactly the same regardless of the number of stocks"], 2, "Diversification removes stock-specific risk, but systematic market risk remains. Risk therefore declines and then levels off at the market risk floor."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "A key assumption of CAPM that is often unrealistic is that:", ["Investors care about both risk and return", "Securities markets have many participants", "Investors can borrow and lend at the risk-free rate", "Diversification reduces unsystematic risk"], 2, "CAPM assumes unlimited borrowing and lending at a single risk-free rate, no taxes or transaction costs and homogeneous expectations. In practice borrowing rates exceed lending rates."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "The Fama-French three-factor model adds which two factors to the market factor?", ["Inflation and GDP growth across countries", "Dividend yield and the number of shareholders", "Interest rates and currency exchange rates", "Size and value"], 3, "Fama and French found that small companies and high book-to-market (value) stocks earned returns not explained by beta. Their model adds size (SMB) and value (HML) factors."], ["Behavioral Finance", "The 'endowment effect' leads investors to:", ["Sell inherited shares immediately to diversify", "Prefer to buy assets that others are selling", "Value assets they own more than identical ones", "Ignore how much they paid for an asset"], 2, "People demand more to give up something they own than they would pay to acquire it. Clients may refuse to sell inherited or long-held shares even when diversification is needed."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio of ₹80 lakh grows to ₹96 lakh. The hurdle rate is 10% and the performance fee is 20% of returns above the hurdle (no catch-up). The performance fee is:", ["₹1.6 lakh", "₹3.2 lakh", "₹0.8 lakh", "₹1.92 lakh"], 0, "Hurdle value = 80 × 1.10 = ₹88 lakh. Excess = 96 − 88 = ₹8 lakh. Fee = 20% × 8 = ₹1.6 lakh. Charging 20% on the full ₹16 lakh gain would give ₹3.2 lakh."], ["Equity Portfolio Management Strategies", "A GARP (growth at a reasonable price) investor typically looks for stocks with:", ["The highest P/E ratios in the market regardless of growth", "Falling earnings and very high dividend payouts", "Good growth at moderate valuations", "Prices that have fallen the most in the last month"], 2, "GARP blends growth and value, seeking firms with solid earnings growth that are not overpriced. A common metric is the PEG ratio (P/E divided by growth)."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "The risk-free rate is 7%, the market's expected return is 13% and its standard deviation is 15%. A portfolio on the CML with 10% standard deviation has an expected return of:", ["13%", "11%", "9%", "15%"], 1, "CML: E(R) = Rf + [(Rm − Rf) / σm] × σp = 7 + (6 / 15) × 10 = 7 + 4 = 11%. The slope 0.4 is the market's Sharpe ratio."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Under SEBI rules, can a portfolio manager borrow funds on behalf of a client to buy securities?", ["Yes, up to two times the client's portfolio value", "Yes, if the distributor approves the borrowing", "No, PMS client portfolios cannot be leveraged", "Yes, but only for buying unlisted shares"], 2, "SEBI prohibits portfolio managers from leveraging client portfolios for investment, protecting clients from magnified losses. Derivatives use is also restricted to permitted purposes."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "Which document must a company file for an IPO that gives investors detailed information about the issue?", ["The offer document or prospectus", "The portfolio manager's disclosure document for its PMS clients", "The company's annual GST return filed with the tax department", "The depository's statement of holdings for promoters"], 0, "Issuers disclose business, financials, risks and issue terms through the draft and final offer documents filed with SEBI and the exchanges. Investors should read them before applying."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "An event study examining share prices around earnings announcements is used to test:", ["How quickly prices absorb new information", "Whether a company's auditors are independent", "How many shares the promoters have pledged", "Whether a company pays tax on time"], 0, "Event studies measure abnormal returns around events. In an efficient market, prices adjust quickly and fully, leaving no predictable abnormal returns afterwards."], ["Investing in Stocks", "Return on capital employed (ROCE) differs from ROE mainly because ROCE:", ["Uses net profit after tax divided only by shareholders' equity", "Ignores debt and measures only the dividend paid per share", "Is calculated only for banks and insurance companies", "Uses operating profit on equity plus debt"], 3, "ROCE = EBIT ÷ (equity + debt), showing returns on all capital in the business. ROE uses net profit on equity only and can be inflated by leverage."], ["Investment Landscape", "A high-net-worth investor chooses PMS over a mutual fund mainly to obtain:", ["A guaranteed return that is higher than any mutual fund scheme can offer", "A customised portfolio held in her own name", "Exemption from all taxes on capital gains made in the portfolio", "The ability to invest with a ticket size of just ₹5,000"], 1, "PMS offers individual portfolios, direct ownership and tailoring to the client's needs. It carries no guarantee, no tax exemption and requires a minimum of ₹50 lakh."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "A positive Jensen's alpha indicates that the portfolio:", ["Had lower standard deviation than the market portfolio", "Had a beta of exactly 1.0 during the period", "Charged no fees to its investors", "Earned more than CAPM predicts for its beta"], 3, "Jensen's alpha = actual return − CAPM required return. A positive value suggests the manager added value beyond what systematic risk would explain."], ["Equity Portfolio Management Strategies", "Why does high portfolio turnover often hurt net returns in PMS?", ["It guarantees that the manager will beat the benchmark", "It reduces the management fee charged to clients", "It raises transaction costs and short-term taxes", "It lowers the portfolio's beta automatically"], 2, "Frequent trading adds brokerage, impact cost and STT, and creates short-term capital gains taxed in the client's hands. Gains from active trading must exceed these costs."], ["Investment Landscape", "An investor in the 'accumulation' phase of the life cycle typically:", ["Draws down savings to meet everyday living expenses after retirement", "Transfers wealth to heirs through wills and trusts as the main priority", "Builds wealth with a long horizon and steady income", "Holds almost all wealth in cash because no goals remain"], 2, "The accumulation phase covers working years, when income exceeds spending and the horizon is long, allowing more growth assets. Drawing down and wealth transfer belong to later stages."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "If markets are semi-strong form efficient, which approach should NOT earn consistent excess returns?", ["Fundamental analysis of public information", "Trading on genuine non-public inside information", "Holding a low-cost index fund to match market returns", "Diversifying across many securities to reduce risk"], 0, "Semi-strong efficiency means prices reflect all public information, so analysing published data cannot reliably beat the market. Only private information could, and trading on it is illegal."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "Strategic asset allocation for a client is primarily determined by:", ["Next month's market outlook given by a television analyst", "The asset class with the highest return over the past six months", "Long-term objectives, risk profile and constraints", "The portfolio manager's personal investment preferences"], 2, "Strategic allocation is the long-term policy mix based on the client's profile. Short-term market views belong to tactical allocation within permitted ranges."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "Shares of a listed company that are pledged by promoters are a concern to portfolio managers mainly because:", ["Pledged shares cannot be counted in the company's market capitalisation at all", "SEBI bans the company from paying dividends while any pledge exists", "Pledged shares automatically convert into debt of the company", "Forced selling by lenders can hit the share price"], 3, "If the share price falls, lenders may invoke the pledge and sell, adding to downward pressure. High promoter pledging is a governance and risk signal analysts watch."], ["Investment Landscape", "An investor's nominal portfolio return is 10% and inflation is 6.5%. The approximate real return is:", ["16.5%", "6.5%", "1.5%", "3.5%"], 3, "Approximate real return = nominal return − inflation = 10 − 6.5 = 3.5%. The exact figure is 1.10 / 1.065 − 1 ≈ 3.3%, slightly lower than the approximation."], ["Risk", "A portfolio has a 95% one-day VaR of ₹8 lakh. Which statement is correct?", ["The portfolio will never lose more than ₹8 lakh on any single day", "The portfolio is expected to lose ₹8 lakh on 95% of days", "There is a 5% chance of losing more than ₹8 lakh in a day", "The portfolio's expected daily gain is ₹8 lakh"], 2, "VaR at 95% gives the loss level exceeded on about 5% of days. It does not cap losses, and it says nothing about how large losses are beyond that level."], ["Mutual Fund", "A key difference between a mutual fund and a PMS for the investor is that, in a mutual fund:", ["Each trade made by the fund manager is taxed in the investor's hands", "Tax arises only when the investor redeems units", "The investor's minimum investment is ₹50 lakh in all cases", "The investor can instruct the fund manager on which stocks to buy"], 1, "Mutual fund trades inside the scheme do not create tax for unitholders; tax arises on redemption or transfer of units. In PMS, each sale is taxed in the client's hands."], ["Risk", "Concentration risk in a portfolio arises when:", ["A large share is in one stock, sector or issuer", "The portfolio holds a broad mix across many sectors and asset classes", "The client invests a small amount through monthly SIPs", "The portfolio is benchmarked against a total return index"], 0, "Heavy exposure to a single security, issuer or sector means one adverse event can cause large losses. Diversification and limits help manage this risk."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Operating expenses charged to a PMS client, excluding brokerage, are:", ["Unlimited, provided they are mentioned somewhere in the agreement", "Capped by SEBI as a percentage of average AUM", "Paid fully by the distributor, never by the client", "Charged only when the client's portfolio makes a loss"], 1, "SEBI caps operating expenses (excluding brokerage) as a percentage of the client's average daily AUM. This prevents managers from loading clients with unlimited costs."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio manager's marketing brochure shows only the best five years of performance. This is a problem because:", ["Selective performance can mislead investors", "SEBI requires brochures to show only the worst years", "Performance can never be shown in marketing material", "Five years is always too short a period to be legal"], 0, "Advertisements must be fair and not misleading. Showing cherry-picked periods hides the full record; standardised, complete performance reporting is expected."], ["Fixed Income Portfolio Management Strategies", "A barbell portfolio holds bonds concentrated at:", ["One single intermediate maturity", "Short and long maturities, with little in between", "Evenly spread maturities every year", "Only perpetual bonds"], 1, "A barbell combines short-term and long-term bonds. A bullet concentrates around one maturity, while a ladder spreads maturities evenly over time."], ["Fixed Income Portfolio Management Strategies", "A manager buys AA corporate bonds expecting the spread over G-secs to narrow as the economy improves. This is a:", ["Credit spread strategy", "Pure duration strategy based on rate falls", "Cash-flow matching strategy for pensions", "Passive indexing strategy"], 0, "Credit strategies take positions on changes in credit spreads. If the economy improves and default risk falls, spreads narrow and the bonds outperform G-secs."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "The Capital Market Line uses which measure of risk on its horizontal axis?", ["Beta of the security relative to the market", "Duration of the bond portfolio", "Standard deviation of portfolio returns", "Dividend yield of the market index"], 2, "The CML plots efficient portfolios combining the risk-free asset and the market portfolio, so it uses total risk (standard deviation). The SML uses beta."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio manager's 'suitability' obligation requires it to:", ["Offer every client the strategy with the highest past return", "Accept any client who meets the minimum investment, whatever their needs", "Ensure that no client portfolio ever shows a loss", "Match strategies to the client's risk profile and goals"], 3, "Suitability means recommending only what fits the client's financial situation, risk tolerance and objectives. Past returns or minimum eligibility alone do not make a product suitable."], ["Derivatives", "A portfolio manager expects a stock to stay in a narrow range until expiry. Which strategy best fits this view?", ["Selling a strangle", "Buying a straddle at the current market price", "Buying out-of-the-money call options only", "Buying the stock's futures with full leverage"], 0, "Selling out-of-the-money call and put options earns premium if the stock stays within the range. Buying a straddle profits from big moves, the opposite view. Short strangles carry large risk if the range breaks."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "A client inherits ₹2 crore and has no immediate need for the money. Before investing, the portfolio manager should:", ["Update the client's profile and IPS", "Invest the full amount immediately in the existing strategy without review", "Advise the client to keep the money in a savings account permanently", "Invest all of it in a single high-conviction stock"], 0, "A major change in wealth can change risk capacity, goals and constraints. The manager should reassess the profile and update the IPS before deploying the new money."], ["Behavioral Finance", "A fund manager who credits gains to his skill but blames losses on bad luck is showing:", ["Anchoring to a historical purchase price", "Self-attribution bias", "Loss aversion causing him to hold losers", "The disposition effect in his tax planning"], 1, "Self-attribution bias leads people to take credit for successes and deflect failures. Over time it feeds overconfidence and can lead to excessive risk-taking."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "A bond has modified duration 6 and convexity effects can be ignored. If its yield rises from 7.0% to 7.5%, its price changes by approximately:", ["+3%", "−6%", "−3%", "−0.5%"], 2, "Price change ≈ −modified duration × change in yield = −6 × 0.5% = −3%. Prices move opposite to yields, scaled by duration."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "The growth of passive investing is often supported by the argument that:", ["Index funds guarantee higher returns than all active funds every year", "SEBI has banned active management for large investors", "Passive funds do not carry any market risk", "Most active funds fail to beat benchmarks after costs"], 3, "If markets are reasonably efficient, active managers on average match the market before costs and lag after costs. Passive funds still carry full market risk."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A PMS client wants to close the account and receive her securities instead of cash. The portfolio manager should:", ["Insist on selling all securities and paying only cash", "Transfer the securities to her own demat account", "Hold the securities until the next financial year", "Transfer the securities to the distributor's account"], 1, "Clients may choose to receive securities in specie on closure. The manager must arrange transfer to the client's own demat account after settling dues as per the agreement."], ["Investment Landscape", "Financialisation of household savings in India refers to:", ["A shift from mutual funds back to gold jewellery and land", "The government taking control of all household bank deposits", "A shift from physical assets to financial assets", "Banks converting savings accounts into fixed deposits automatically"], 2, "Indian households have traditionally held much wealth in gold and real estate. Financialisation describes the growing share of savings going into deposits, mutual funds, equities, insurance and pensions."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "The 'momentum anomaly' refers to the tendency for:", ["Stocks with low trading volume to always rise in price", "Recent winners to keep outperforming for a while", "All stocks to fall on the first trading day of the month", "Large companies to always outperform small companies"], 1, "Studies show stocks with strong recent returns tend to keep outperforming over the following months. This challenges weak-form efficiency, though risk or behaviour may explain it."], ["Mutual Fund", "An investor puts ₹12,000 into a scheme at NAV ₹40 and redeems all units when NAV is ₹46, with no loads. The absolute return is:", ["6%", "13%", "20%", "15%"], 3, "Units = 12,000 ÷ 40 = 300. Redemption value = 300 × 46 = ₹13,800. Absolute return = 1,800 ÷ 12,000 = 15%, the same as (46 − 40) ÷ 40."], ["Taxation", "When a client transfers existing shares into a PMS account (no sale), the transfer generally:", ["Is not a taxable sale", "Triggers long-term capital gains tax on the full value", "Is treated as a gift taxable in the manager's hands", "Requires payment of STT at the time of transfer"], 0, "Moving securities into a PMS account held in the client's own name does not change ownership, so there is no sale and no capital gain. Original cost and dates carry over."], ["Derivatives", "Initial margin on a futures position is meant to:", ["Pay the full contract value upfront to the seller", "Cover potential one-day losses on the position", "Act as brokerage paid to the trading member", "Provide a guaranteed return on the investor's capital"], 1, "Initial margin is collateral sized to cover likely adverse moves, typically based on risk models. Futures require only a fraction of the contract value, which creates leverage."], ["Taxation", "A client sells shares and immediately buys them back mainly to book a loss for tax purposes. A portfolio manager should note that:", ["Tax and anti-avoidance rules may apply", "The loss can always be claimed with no conditions whatsoever", "Such trades are banned by SEBI in all circumstances", "The client will automatically receive a refund of all past taxes"], 0, "Tax-loss harvesting can be legitimate, but transactions mainly designed to avoid tax may be examined under tax rules. Clients should seek professional tax advice."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "A callable bond is less attractive to investors than a similar non-callable bond because:", ["The investor must hold it until maturity without any coupon payments", "It always carries a lower credit rating than non-callable bonds", "Its coupons are taxed at a higher rate than other bonds", "The issuer may redeem it early when rates fall"], 3, "Issuers call bonds when rates fall, forcing investors to reinvest at lower yields and capping price gains. Investors therefore demand a higher yield for callable bonds."], ["Mutual Fund", "Under SEBI's scheme categorisation, a flexi-cap fund must invest at least:", ["25% each in large, mid and small-cap stocks at all times", "80% in large-cap stocks and the rest in debt", "65% in equity across market caps", "100% in mid-cap and small-cap stocks only"], 2, "Flexi-cap funds must keep at least 65% in equity with freedom across market caps. Multi-cap funds, in contrast, must hold at least 25% each in large, mid and small caps."], ["Investment Landscape", "Which product would generally NOT be considered a market-linked investment?", ["A bank fixed deposit", "An equity-oriented mutual fund scheme bought through SIP", "Units of a listed REIT traded on the exchange", "A discretionary PMS equity strategy"], 0, "A fixed deposit pays a contracted rate regardless of market movement. Mutual fund units, REIT units and PMS portfolios fluctuate with market prices."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "The optimal portfolio for a particular investor in MPT is found where:", ["The efficient frontier meets the horizontal axis at zero risk", "The portfolio has the highest return regardless of its risk", "The portfolio contains the largest number of assets possible", "Her indifference curve touches the efficient frontier"], 3, "Each investor picks the efficient portfolio that maximises her utility, at the point where her highest attainable indifference curve is tangent to the frontier."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A company is expected to pay a dividend of ₹6 next year, growing at 8% forever. If the required return is 14%, its value per share under the Gordon growth model is:", ["₹75", "₹100", "₹42.86", "₹108"], 1, "Value = D1 / (r − g) = 6 / (0.14 − 0.08) = 6 / 0.06 = ₹100. The model works only when the required return exceeds the growth rate."], ["Behavioral Finance", "Regret aversion can cause investors to:", ["Trade much more frequently to capture every opportunity", "Diversify widely across many asset classes and countries", "Rebalance strictly every quarter as per the IPS", "Avoid action to escape the pain of a wrong decision"], 3, "Fear of regret may lead investors to delay decisions, stick to familiar choices or follow the crowd. This can leave portfolios unbalanced or in unsuitable assets."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "Why should portfolio performance generally be shown net of fees to clients?", ["Gross returns are always lower than net returns", "SEBI does not allow fees to be disclosed", "Clients actually receive net returns", "Fees have no effect on long-term returns"], 2, "Fees and expenses directly reduce investor outcomes, so net-of-fee performance reflects the client's experience. Gross figures can overstate the value delivered."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "A bond with face value ₹1,000 and coupon 8% is trading at ₹1,050. Which statement is correct?", ["Its YTM is below 8%", "Its YTM is above 8% because the price is above face value", "Its current yield equals its coupon rate of exactly 8%", "It is trading at a discount to its face value"], 0, "A bond priced above face value is at a premium, so the buyer gets less than the coupon rate as yield. Current yield = 80 ÷ 1,050 ≈ 7.6%, also below 8%."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "The credit spread on a corporate bond is the:", ["Difference between the bond's coupon and the RBI repo rate", "Brokerage paid when buying the bond in the secondary market", "Gap between the bond's face value and its issue price", "Extra yield over a comparable government bond"], 3, "Credit spread compensates for default and liquidity risk relative to a risk-free G-sec of similar maturity. Spreads widen when credit risk is perceived to rise."], ["Equity Portfolio Management Strategies", "An index fund that holds only a representative subset of index stocks to reduce costs is using:", ["Full replication of every constituent in exact index weights", "Sector rotation based on an economic outlook", "Stratified sampling", "A concentrated high-conviction approach"], 2, "Sampling selects stocks so that the portfolio matches key index characteristics (sector, size, factor exposure) without holding every constituent. It may add some tracking error."], ["Taxation", "GST on portfolio management fees is borne by:", ["The custodian, from its own revenue", "The client, as part of fees charged", "SEBI, as part of investor protection", "The companies whose shares are held in the portfolio"], 1, "Portfolio management is a taxable service, so GST is levied on management and performance fees and collected from the client along with the fee."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A 'moving average crossover' signal is a tool of:", ["Technical analysis", "Fundamental analysis of the company's balance sheet and cash flows", "Credit analysis of the company's bonds by rating agencies", "Macroeconomic analysis of the country's current account"], 0, "Technical analysts use price-based indicators such as moving averages; a short-term average crossing above a long-term one is read as bullish. Fundamental analysis studies business value."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio manager registered with SEBI can accept as a client:", ["Only resident individuals, never companies, trusts or NRIs", "Only investors who already hold a mutual fund account", "Only investors recommended by a stock exchange", "Any eligible investor meeting PMS norms"], 3, "PMS is open to individuals, HUFs, companies, trusts, NRIs and others who meet KYC and minimum investment norms. There is no requirement to hold mutual funds or be referred by an exchange."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "Why might apparent market anomalies disappear after they are published?", ["SEBI bans trading in stocks linked to the anomaly", "Stock exchanges stop publishing prices of those stocks", "Companies change their accounting year to remove them", "Investors trade on them and arbitrage them away"], 3, "Once an anomaly is widely known, investors exploit it, which moves prices and removes the excess return. Some anomalies may also have been data-mining results."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "Accredited investors in PMS may be offered:", ["Lower minimums under the large value framework", "Guaranteed returns linked to the repo rate", "Exemption from KYC and anti-money laundering checks", "Permission to use unlimited leverage on their accounts"], 0, "SEBI allows portfolio managers to offer accredited investors flexibilities such as relaxation of the ₹50 lakh minimum under the large value accredited investor framework. KYC and leverage rules still apply."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "A key practical limitation of mean-variance optimisation is that:", ["It cannot handle portfolios with more than two assets", "Outputs are very sensitive to estimated inputs", "It ignores expected returns entirely and uses only prices", "It requires all assets to have exactly the same risk"], 1, "Small changes in expected returns or correlations can produce very different 'optimal' weights, often extreme. Practitioners use constraints or robust methods to handle estimation error."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "A stock rises sharply over three days after positive results are announced, rather than adjusting immediately. This pattern suggests:", ["Perfect strong-form efficiency in the market", "Slow reaction, inconsistent with semi-strong efficiency", "That insider trading must have occurred before the results", "That the company's results were not public information"], 1, "Post-announcement drift shows that prices take time to absorb public news, which semi-strong efficiency rules out. It is a well-documented anomaly."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio returned 1.5% per month for 12 months. Its annualised return (compounded) is approximately:", ["18.0%", "12.0%", "19.6%", "21.0%"], 2, "Annual return = (1.015)^12 − 1 ≈ 19.56%. Simply multiplying 1.5% by 12 gives 18%, which ignores the effect of compounding monthly returns over the year."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A firm's ROE is 18% and it pays out 40% of earnings as dividends. Its sustainable growth rate is approximately:", ["7.2%", "10.8%", "18.0%", "22.0%"], 1, "Sustainable growth = ROE × retention ratio = 18% × (1 − 0.40) = 18% × 0.60 = 10.8%. Retained earnings reinvested at the ROE drive growth without new equity."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "Convexity is valuable to a bond investor because, for large yield changes, it means:", ["Prices fall more and rise less than duration alone would suggest", "The bond's coupon automatically resets to market rates", "The bond becomes free of all credit and liquidity risk", "Prices rise more and fall less than duration predicts"], 3, "Duration is a linear estimate. Positive convexity curves the price–yield line so actual gains exceed and losses fall short of the duration estimate for big moves."], ["Mutual Fund", "In India, the entity that holds mutual fund assets in trust for unitholders is the:", ["Board of trustees or trustee company", "Asset management company that runs the investment portfolio", "Registrar and transfer agent that maintains investor records", "Stock exchange where the fund's trades are executed"], 0, "Mutual funds are set up as trusts. Trustees hold assets for unitholders and oversee the AMC, which manages the money. RTAs and exchanges provide services but do not own assets."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A client's portfolio is ₹1.20 crore at the end of year 1 (high-water mark) and ₹1.10 crore at the end of year 2. A 20% performance fee over the high-water mark in year 2 is:", ["₹2 lakh", "₹4 lakh", "Nil", "₹22 lakh"], 2, "The year-2 value is below the high-water mark of ₹1.20 crore, so no new gains exist and no performance fee is payable. Fees resume only after the portfolio exceeds ₹1.20 crore."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "A portfolio holds 40% in asset A (expected return 12%) and 60% in asset B (expected return 8%). Its expected return is:", ["10.0%", "9.6%", "10.4%", "20.0%"], 1, "Expected portfolio return is the weighted average: 0.4 × 12% + 0.6 × 8% = 4.8% + 4.8% = 9.6%. Unlike risk, expected return does not depend on correlation."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "A client asks a discretionary portfolio manager to avoid all stocks in the alcohol sector. The manager should:", ["Ignore it, because discretionary managers decide everything freely", "Refuse to accept the client because restrictions are not allowed", "Move the client to a mutual fund without explaining why", "Record the restriction and follow it"], 3, "Discretionary authority operates within the client agreement. Client-specific restrictions should be documented and followed, as customisation is a key feature of PMS."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "Two assets each have standard deviation 10%, and the correlation between them is +1. A 50:50 portfolio has standard deviation:", ["5%", "7.07%", "0%", "10%"], 3, "With perfect positive correlation there is no diversification benefit, so portfolio risk is the weighted average: 10%. With zero correlation it would be about 7.07%."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Employees of a portfolio manager who learn of large client orders and trade ahead of them personally are guilty of:", ["Legitimate personal investment based on their own research", "Portfolio rebalancing as per the client's IPS", "Front-running", "Tax-loss harvesting for their own benefit"], 2, "Front-running exploits knowledge of pending client orders for personal gain, harming clients. SEBI treats it as a fraudulent practice punishable under the PFUTP regulations."], ["Equity Portfolio Management Strategies", "A market-neutral equity strategy aims to:", ["Earn returns independent of market direction", "Deliver returns exactly equal to the market index", "Hold only long positions in high-beta stocks", "Maximise exposure to the overall market"], 0, "Market-neutral strategies balance long and short positions so that net beta is near zero. Returns come from stock selection rather than market movement."], ["Mutual Fund", "The riskometer of a mutual fund scheme is:", ["Fixed at launch and never changed for the life of the scheme", "Decided each year by the investors through a vote", "Reviewed monthly based on the scheme's portfolio", "Shown only to institutional investors and not to retail investors"], 2, "SEBI requires AMCs to evaluate the riskometer monthly using portfolio-based parameters and disclose it, so investors can see changes in scheme risk."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "Composite reporting of a PMS strategy's performance means:", ["Combining all accounts managed under that strategy", "Showing only the single best-performing account", "Reporting the average of all strategies of every manager in India", "Using only accounts opened in the last month"], 0, "Composites group all fee-paying discretionary accounts in a strategy, preventing cherry-picking. They give clients a representative picture of the strategy's results."], ["Fixed Income Portfolio Management Strategies", "An investor who expects a flattening of the yield curve (long yields falling relative to short yields) would prefer a:", ["Barbell over a bullet of the same duration", "Bullet over a barbell of the same duration", "Portfolio of only T-bills", "Portfolio of only floating-rate notes"], 0, "Barbells have more convexity and more exposure to long maturities. When the curve flattens, the long bonds in the barbell gain, so the barbell tends to outperform a bullet."], ["Equity Portfolio Management Strategies", "An enhanced indexing strategy aims to:", ["Earn returns several times higher than the index with high leverage", "Track the index exactly with zero difference in returns", "Hold only cash and short-term bonds", "Beat the index modestly with low tracking error"], 3, "Enhanced indexing makes small, controlled deviations from the benchmark — such as factor tilts — to add modest excess return while keeping tracking error low."], ["Fixed Income Portfolio Management Strategies", "'Riding the yield curve' is a strategy that:", ["Buys only the shortest maturity T-bills and rolls them weekly", "Holds bonds exactly to maturity in all cases", "Invests only in bonds with a flat yield curve", "Buys bonds longer than the horizon and sells as they roll down an upward curve"], 3, "With an upward-sloping curve, as a bond ages its yield falls along the curve and its price rises. Selling before maturity captures this extra roll-down gain, if the curve stays stable."], ["Risk", "A portfolio's returns over four years were 10%, −5%, 15% and 0%. The arithmetic mean return is:", ["5.0%", "4.5%", "7.5%", "20.0%"], 0, "Arithmetic mean = (10 − 5 + 15 + 0) ÷ 4 = 20 ÷ 4 = 5%. The geometric mean, which reflects compounding, would be slightly lower."], ["Equity Portfolio Management Strategies", "A stock has a P/E of 24 and expected earnings growth of 16%. Its PEG ratio is:", ["0.67", "3.84", "8.0", "1.5"], 3, "PEG = P/E ÷ growth rate (in percentage points) = 24 ÷ 16 = 1.5. Many GARP investors see a PEG near or below 1 as attractive."], ["Portfolio Rebalancing", "A CPPI strategy with a floor of ₹80 lakh, portfolio value ₹1 crore and multiplier 3 would invest in equity:", ["₹20 lakh", "₹60 lakh", "₹80 lakh", "₹100 lakh"], 1, "Cushion = 100 − 80 = ₹20 lakh. Equity = multiplier × cushion = 3 × 20 = ₹60 lakh. The rest, ₹40 lakh, goes into safe assets."], ["Introduction to Indices", "Smart-beta or factor indices differ from traditional cap-weighted indices because they:", ["Always contain exactly the same stocks and weights as the Nifty 50", "Weight stocks by factors such as value or quality", "Are calculated only once every five years by the stock exchange", "Include only government securities and no equities at all"], 1, "Factor indices use rules based on characteristics like value, momentum, low volatility or quality to select or weight stocks, aiming to capture specific return drivers."], ["Portfolio Rebalancing", "Why might a manager decide not to rebalance a small drift of 1% from target?", ["Rebalancing is not permitted under SEBI rules", "Small drifts always correct themselves the next day", "The client's IPS never mentions target weights", "Costs and taxes may exceed the benefit"], 3, "Each rebalance incurs transaction costs and possibly taxes. If drift is small, the risk reduction gained may not justify those costs; bands help manage this trade-off."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio manager's conflict of interest policy should cover situations such as:", ["The colour scheme used in the company's office", "The number of holidays given to employees", "The location of the registered office", "Investing client money in associate companies"], 3, "Conflicts arise where the manager or associates benefit at clients' expense, such as investing in associates or allocating trades unfairly. The policy must identify, disclose and manage them."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "During the feedback stage of portfolio management, the manager mainly:", ["Opens the client's demat and bank accounts for the first time", "Collects KYC documents from the client", "Reviews results against objectives and adjusts", "Signs the PMS agreement with the client"], 2, "Feedback involves measuring performance, comparing it with objectives and benchmarks, and adjusting the portfolio or IPS. Account opening and KYC happen at the start."], ["Taxation", "For capital gains purposes, the holding period of shares bought through PMS is counted from:", ["The date the client signed the PMS agreement with the manager", "The start of the financial year in which the shares were bought", "The date the portfolio manager obtained SEBI registration", "The date the client acquired each share"], 3, "Each security in the client's name has its own acquisition date, which determines whether a gain is short-term or long-term. The agreement date or financial year start is irrelevant."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "An investment policy statement (IPS) is most useful for:", ["Replacing the need for any client agreement with the manager", "Guaranteeing that the portfolio will meet its target return", "Allowing the manager to change the strategy without client input", "Guiding decisions and evaluating the manager"], 3, "The IPS documents objectives, constraints and allocation, giving a reference for decisions and reviews. It does not guarantee returns or replace legal agreements."], ["Portfolio Rebalancing", "Using fresh client inflows to buy underweight assets instead of selling overweight ones helps to:", ["Increase the portfolio's turnover and short-term gains", "Rebalance with fewer costs and taxes", "Avoid ever reviewing the portfolio's allocation", "Guarantee higher returns than the benchmark"], 1, "Directing new money to underweight assets moves the portfolio towards target without selling, reducing brokerage and avoiding capital gains taxes."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "Novation by a clearing corporation means it:", ["Becomes the counterparty to both buyer and seller", "Chooses which brokers are allowed to trade on a particular day", "Transfers shares directly from the company to the buyer", "Sets the issue price for initial public offerings"], 0, "Novation replaces the original contract between buyer and seller with two contracts with the clearing corporation, which removes counterparty risk between trading members."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "Under CAPM, a stock with a beta of zero should earn:", ["The risk-free rate", "The market return", "Zero return", "Twice the risk-free rate"], 0, "With beta zero, the stock has no systematic risk, so CAPM gives Rf + 0 × (Rm − Rf) = Rf. Its own volatility does not earn a premium if it is diversifiable."], ["Taxation", "For a non-resident PMS client, which is generally the portfolio manager's responsibility in relation to capital gains?", ["Paying the client's tax from its own net worth", "Deciding the client's country of tax residence", "Claiming treaty benefits without any documents", "Facilitating TDS as required by law"], 3, "For NRIs, tax must be deducted at source on capital gains, and the manager or its custodian facilitates this. Treaty benefits need documents such as a tax residency certificate."], ["Risk", "Operational risk for a portfolio manager includes:", ["Losses from failed processes, systems or people", "Losses from a general fall in equity markets across the country", "Changes in interest rates that affect bond prices", "Changes in the exchange rate of the rupee"], 0, "Operational risk covers errors, fraud, system failures and process breakdowns, such as wrong trade entries. Market, rate and currency risks are separate categories."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "A stock's covariance with the market is 0.03 and the market's variance is 0.025. The stock's beta is:", ["1.2", "0.83", "0.75", "0.03"], 0, "Beta = covariance(stock, market) ÷ variance(market) = 0.03 ÷ 0.025 = 1.2. A beta above 1 means the stock tends to move more than the market."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A company has EV of ₹1,200 crore and EBITDA of ₹150 crore. Its EV/EBITDA multiple is:", ["12×", "8×", "6×", "10×"], 1, "EV/EBITDA = 1,200 ÷ 150 = 8. This multiple is useful for comparing companies with different capital structures, because EV includes debt and EBITDA is before interest."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "Under the 2020 Regulations, the principal officer of a portfolio manager must have:", ["Only a degree in any discipline without any work experience", "Required qualification, experience and NISM certification", "At least 25 years of experience as a stock broker", "A licence from the Reserve Bank of India"], 1, "SEBI specifies minimum professional qualifications, relevant experience and NISM certification for the principal officer, who is responsible for investment decisions and operations."], ["Fixed Income Portfolio Management Strategies", "A manager expects interest rates to fall sharply. An appropriate active strategy is to:", ["Shorten portfolio duration to near zero", "Sell all bonds and hold only cash", "Buy only floating-rate bonds", "Lengthen portfolio duration"], 3, "Bond prices rise when rates fall, and longer-duration bonds gain more. Shortening duration or holding floaters would reduce the benefit of falling rates."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A bottom-up stock selection approach begins with:", ["A forecast of GDP growth, inflation and interest rates for the economy", "Choosing the best-performing sectors for the next two years first", "Selecting a benchmark index and copying its weights exactly", "Analysis of individual companies"], 3, "Bottom-up investors focus on company fundamentals first, regardless of economic or sector views. Top-down investing starts from the economy and then narrows to sectors and stocks."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "Covariance between two assets' returns measures:", ["How their returns move together", "The average return of the two assets combined over time", "The difference between their highest and lowest prices", "The dividend yield of the combined portfolio"], 0, "Covariance shows whether returns tend to move in the same direction. Correlation standardises covariance by dividing by the product of standard deviations, giving a value between −1 and +1."], ["Portfolio Rebalancing", "Threshold (range-based) rebalancing means rebalancing:", ["On a fixed date every year regardless of the portfolio's drift", "Only when the client requests it in writing", "When weights drift beyond set bands", "Every day to keep exact target weights"], 2, "Range-based rebalancing acts when an asset's weight moves beyond limits, such as ±5%. It ties trading to actual drift rather than the calendar."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "Which activity falls outside the scope of a portfolio manager's services?", ["Managing a client's equity portfolio on a discretionary basis", "Advising a client on securities under an advisory mandate", "Executing trades for a client after approval under non-discretionary PMS", "Accepting deposits from clients with a fixed interest rate"], 3, "Portfolio managers manage or advise on securities portfolios. Accepting deposits at fixed interest is a banking or NBFC activity and is not permitted for portfolio managers."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "In performance attribution, the 'allocation effect' measures the value added by:", ["Picking better stocks within each sector of the benchmark", "Over- or under-weighting sectors versus the benchmark", "Charging lower fees than competing managers", "Reducing the number of client accounts managed"], 1, "Allocation effect captures returns from sector weighting decisions. Selection effect captures returns from choosing stocks within sectors that beat the sector benchmark."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "Strong-form efficiency implies that:", ["Only technical analysis can earn excess returns in the market", "Even insider information cannot earn excess returns", "Public information is not reflected in prices at all", "Fund managers can always beat the index with research"], 1, "Strong-form efficiency means prices reflect all information, public and private. Evidence of insider profits suggests markets are not strong-form efficient."], ["Taxation", "A client has a short-term capital loss from listed shares and a long-term capital gain from listed shares in the same year. The loss:", ["Can be set off only against salary income", "Can be set off against the long-term gain", "Must be carried forward and cannot be used this year", "Is permanently lost if it is not used against short-term gains"], 1, "Short-term capital losses can be set off against both short-term and long-term capital gains in the same year. Long-term losses, however, can only be set off against long-term gains."], ["Introduction to Indices", "Why does an index divisor get adjusted after events like a stock split or constituent change?", ["To keep the index level continuous", "To increase the index value artificially after every corporate action", "To reduce the number of stocks in the index each time", "To reflect the dividend paid by each company in cash"], 0, "Corporate actions and constituent changes alter the aggregate value without any market movement. Adjusting the divisor ensures the index value changes only because of price moves."]]};
